// Test fixtures that need a populated sprite table: d2_spr_init alone leaves
// all five lists empty, so a case reading d2_spr_w(0) or blitting sprite 0
// would index out of range. These push two known sprites so the rest of the
// spr/px and spr/blit cases have real data to read.
//
// id0 "t2" is 2x2, one frame, [5, 9, 7, 3] (row-major: row0 [5, 9], row1
// [7, 3]) -- four distinct, opaque values so a case can tell which corner it
// read. id1 "t3" is 3x1, two frames, [11, -1, 4, 8, 2, -1] -- a second name
// for d2_spr_find, a second size for d2_spr_w/h, a frame count above one for
// the blitters' clamp, and a transparent pixel in each frame.
//
// d2_blit_t_setup composes this with sf_t_setup (given fixture composition):
// the blit functions need both a surface to draw into and a sprite table to
// draw from, and a case names one `given`, so the two are combined here
// rather than in every blit file.

d2_spr_t_setup() {
  d2_spr_init();
  int a = d2_spr_add2("t2", 2, 2, 1, [5, 9, 7, 3]);
  int b = d2_spr_add2("t3", 3, 1, 2, [11, 0 - 1, 4, 8, 2, 0 - 1]);
} return void;
():() then d2_spr_t_w_len:(2)
():() then d2_spr_t_n_len:(2)

d2_blit_t_setup() {
  sf_t_setup();
  d2_spr_t_setup();
} return void;
():() then sf_t_gw:(64)
():() then d2_spr_t_w_len:(2)

// The one surface-pixel probe every blit case reads back: a blit's effect is
// module state (the framebuffer), and a `then` check takes no parameters, so
// this fixes the read at (0, 0) and every blit case chooses its own x/y (and
// which sprite/frame) so that the pixel landing on (0, 0) is the one under
// test.
d2_blit_t_px00() {
  int c = sf_pget(0, 0);
} return int c;
given d2_blit_t_setup ():(0)
given d2_blit_t_setup ():(0) then sf_t_gw:(64)
